            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>Don't go into the fields—so say the village elders—when the sun is highest and the sky is the blue of a robin's egg. That's when the poludnica, the noonday demon, walks the furrows, and to meet them is death.</p>\n<p>Of course, some don't listen. At planting and at harvest, those few extra hours in the middle of the day can mean the difference between making it through the winter and starving to death. Desperation is a powerful force, and while it's just possible some old fireside legend might kill you, hunger most assuredly will. When need isn't at play, greed does a good impression, and pride too. Just a little more work, a little bigger harvest, that little bit extra to impress—who? Yourself? Your spouse? Parents, neighbors, or even your landlord?</p>\n<p>So, some harvesters enter the fields while the sun beats down like a hammer: the desperate, the greedy, and the foolish alike, and if they're lucky, all they have to fear is heat exhaustion, sunstroke, and dehydration. If they're unlucky, they come across a lone stranger in the fields: a young woman dressed in a kaftan of purest white, so bright it hurts to look upon, with eyes blue as cornflowers. Sometimes it's a young man instead, sometimes their clothing is pale blue, sometimes they might appear as a young child or wizened elder. Always they hold a scythe, a sickle, or a pair of old pruning shears.</p>\n<p>The poludnica asks questions—they're fond of riddles and trick questions, confusing nonsense—all while the sun glares and the fey twists in your sight like a mirage. Answer wrongly, or stay silent, and she'll clout you on the head, causing headaches or madness. Or perhaps they'll simply bring that sickle down and slice your head clean off. They'll bury the body then, right there in the fields, and the harvest that rises from that land will be the richest in a hundred years. The people will nod, and the village elders will have a new story to tell, and perhaps in a decade or two, some new fool will die in the fields. And in exchange for their presence, the harvest grows rich and ripe, and hunger stays at bay.</p>\n<p>Poludnicas are proud and territorial fey—a deadly threat, but a manageable one on the whole, if you're wise and listen to those who know better. They guard their fields and their time closely, and view outsiders as trespassers to be dealt with. At the same time, poludnicas don't actually hate their mortal neighbors. A poludnica's relationship with \"their\" village has a parental bent, that of a shepherd to their sheep (including taking the occasional meal of mutton). A poludnica ensures the harvest is good and locals don't starve, which sometimes means culling the occasional fool who fails to show respect. No one's entirely sure what poludnicas get in the exchange, though. Mortals say that poludnicas dally with mortals, ensuring their line is continued onward. Given that, like all fey, poludnicas are immortal, this is likely just some farmhand's wishful thinking. Other fey simply think that poludnicas are all stuck-up and enjoy having someone in mortal terror of them. This is more plausible.</p>\n<p>There's an old story that says that Poludnica—just a single fey in the story—was one of four sisters: the midday star. Beside her was the youngest, Zarya, the dawn star, the evening star, Vechernitsa, and the midnight star, Nochnitsa. Together, they oversaw the proper course of the hours and the transition from darkness to light and back. They rarely quarreled, though rarely saw eye to eye, either. Supposedly, a mortal caught out by a poludnica should suggest they're on an errand for one of their sisters, especially Nochnitsa, whom Poludnica never sees. The fey might suspect a ruse, but they're likelier to let the mortal go on their way than risk aggravating their sisters.</p>\n<p>Another story claims that the noonday demons love dancing and are better at it than any mortal. When the sun is highest and the fields are empty, they dance, alone or in great gatherings. If they meet a mortal during this time, perhaps some farmer dozing the day away, they'll wake them and implore them to dance—on pain of death. A poludnica will dance from noon till dusk, but if the mortal can keep up with them through heat and exhaustion, the impressed fey will reward them richly, with blessings of good fortune, protection, and a purse of gold for good measure.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
        },
